# Design the technical requirements

Turn the accepted business requirements, or the confirmed objectives of a technical change, into
long-lived, measurable technical requirements. Screen every quality attribute, write only the
applicable ones into the feature's `{{vars.techDesignFile}}`, grill them with the user and get them
accepted. State what the system must achieve, never how to build it: the implementation decides that.

## Inputs

- The brief: `## Original request` (context: a number, platform, store or integration it names is
  a candidate requirement the user confirms, `Source: user` once confirmed, its wording never
  copied), the header (`Route`, `Modifiers`, `Tech design sections`, `Context`, `Features`), the
  objectives `OBJ-n` in `## Classification`, `## Quality attribute screening` when re-entered, and
  the latest open `## Rework` entry (`REVISE_DESIGN` from Implement, `DESIGN_GAP` from Dev QA).
- The accepted `{{vars.requirementsFile}}` of each feature and `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/GLOSSARY.md`.
- Each feature's `{{vars.techDesignFile}}` and the baseline `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/{{vars.techDesignFile}}`.
- Adoption or brownfield: the code, manifests and configuration of the touched area.

## Procedure

1. **Read the brief and pick the mode.** Route not `full` or `design`: when a Rework entry sent the
   work here, or the user states design requirements (`ui-design`), propose the corrected route,
   update the header once the user confirms (Task brief section) and continue; otherwise stop and
   say so. Back from a requirements gap: re-apply the `dfl-td-draft` stash (step 7). Address the
   open items you own in the latest Rework entry first and mark them done; keep confirmed verdicts
   and accepted sections unless an open item reopens them. Every mode screens every catalog row.

   | Situation | Draft |
   |---|---|
   | New feature | every section with an Applicable code |
   | Requirements changed, technical change | the brief's `Tech design sections` (`baseline:<key>` is that section of the baseline), plus any the screening adds |
   | `ui-design` | section 5 with strict rows, plus the above |
   | `revert` | restore what the reverted change's tech design acceptance replaced; retire the IDs it added |
   | Re-entered (Rework) | the sections the open items name |

2. **Baseline and constraints.**
   - No baseline yet and the task needs a project-wide item (runtime, hosting, deployment, CI gates,
     code organization): ask consent to create it with `SYS-` IDs; declined: only the feature's file.
   - Greenfield: ask in one message, each with a proposed default (what the request states, if it
     does): runtime and hosting, data store, users and scale, sensitive data, CI host. The answers
     become baseline entries, with the test levels and CI gates the first implementation increment sets up.
   - Adoption or brownfield: extract constraints `CON-n` for the touched area only (manifests,
     compose files, migrations, Dockerfile and IaC, CI workflows, lint and architecture configs,
     logging and metrics setup). Project-wide ones seed the baseline, the rest go to the feature.
     Each names the technology or limit and why it binds, never the file you found it in.
3. **Screen every row of the Quality attribute catalog below**, skipping none: a missing row reads
   as forgotten, and Dev QA checks the diff against these verdicts. Write one table per feature (or
   one for the baseline alone) to the brief's `## Quality attribute screening`: `| Code | Verdict | Reason |`.
   - `Applicable`: this task adds, changes or retires requirements for it; name the sub-characteristics.
   - `Baseline SYS-n`: a baseline requirement covers it; the feature needs nothing of its own.
   - `N/A`: neither; a one-line reason (for an existing feature, the IDs that still hold).

   MAINT (testability) and SEC (integrity) start as Applicable and may only become Baseline. Under
   `sensitive`, ask about SEC, PRIV, SAFE and COMPL explicitly. Present the table in one message
   and get the user's confirmation before drafting.

4. **Draft each applicable section, one at a time,** in the [requirement format](references/requirement-format.md),
   placed per the [document skeleton](references/document-skeleton.md), with measures from
   [quality attributes](references/quality-attributes.md). Open a section guide only for a section
   the screening or the brief put in:

   | Key | Section | Guide | Open when |
   |---|---|---|---|
   | `data` | 3 Data | [data](references/sections/data.md) | an entity, stored attribute, retention or copy of data changes; DCONS or PRIV Applicable |
   | `interfaces` | 4 Interfaces and communication | [interfaces](references/sections/interfaces.md) | an API, event, webhook, file exchange or contract is added or changed |
   | `ui` | 5 UI appearance | [UI](references/sections/ui.md) | a scenario is reached through a UI or CLI and placement changes, or `ui-design` |
   | `operations` | 6 Deployment and operations | [operations](references/sections/operations.md) | REL, FLEX, OBS, DEPLOY or COST need more than the baseline |
   | `engineering` | 7 Engineering | [engineering](references/sections/engineering.md) | MAINT, DX or CICD need more than the baseline |

   Sections 1 (`context`) and 2 (`quality`) need no guide. At most about 3 requirements per
   sub-characteristic; drop any that would not change an implementation choice or a check.

5. **Record decisions only when expensive to reverse,** as `DEC-n` in section 1 (3 to 5 lines:
   context, decision, alternatives, consequences): the system of record and storage when not
   given, sync or async across a boundary, the deployment unit, a public contract and its
   versioning, data ownership and tenancy. Offer options with a recommendation; the user decides.
   Everything else (libraries, patterns, layout, algorithms) is left to the implementation.

6. **Grill each section right after drafting it** with the [grill](references/grill.md), and iterate
   here until the user accepts it. Every requirement gets a priority (Must or Should); every
   conflicting pair goes under Trade-offs in section 2 with the user's choice. In the screening
   section keep `Progress:` (sections accepted) and `Open items:` (questions the user deferred).

7. **Requirements gap.** A business rule or glossary term the design needs is missing or wrong:
   never invent it here. Append a Rework entry (`REQUIREMENTS_GAP`, design-tech, numbered items:
   what is missing, which design item needs it; note "drafts in stash dfl-td-draft"), run
   `git stash push --include-untracked -m dfl-td-draft -- <tech design files>` (on return apply that
   entry of `git stash list`, then drop it), commit only the brief
   (`docs(task): requirements gap for <task>`) and fire `REQUIREMENTS_GAP`.

8. **Write the files.** Edit `{{vars.requirementsDir}}/<feature>/{{vars.techDesignFile}}` in place
   (create it from the skeleton) and the baseline when `SYS-` items change, in the language of the
   requirement files. Reworded IDs keep their ID; replaced or removed ones move to Retired IDs.
   Existing code that misses a requirement never lowers it: ask whether this task meets it or it
   becomes a follow-up (ID and gap) under **Follow-ups and known gaps** in the hand-off.
9. **Get acceptance and commit.** Summarize per file: IDs added, changed and retired, decisions,
   trade-offs, open items, follow-ups. Wait for an explicit yes; silence is not approval. Commit
   only the tech design files (`docs(td): accept <PREFIX> tech design for <task>`; several features
   `CHK, ACC`; the baseline counts as `SYS`; no file changed: `--allow-empty`), write the sha into
   `Accepted: … tech design <sha>` (replacing an older one), add the key of every section you edited
   to `Tech design sections` (`baseline:<key>` for the baseline), commit the brief
   (`docs(task): tech design accepted for <task>`) and fire `NEXT`.

