## Outputs

- Each feature's `{{vars.techDesignFile}}`, and the baseline when `SYS-` items changed: applicable
  requirements only, with constraints, decisions, trade-offs and retired IDs.
- Brief: the screening with progress and open items, follow-ups in the hand-off, the tech design
  sha in `Accepted:`, Rework items marked done. Two commits: tech design files alone, then the brief.

## Exit criteria

- Every catalog code of every feature has a verdict the user confirmed.
- Every drafted section passed the grill and the user accepted it; no `Source: proposed` remains;
  conflicts have a trade-off the user chose; deferred questions are open items, not requirements.
- The user explicitly accepted the summary; the acceptance commit holds only tech design files,
  its sha is in the committed brief, and the tree is clean.
- `REQUIREMENTS_GAP`: the Rework entry is committed in the brief and drafts are stashed by name.

## Pitfalls

- Implementation in disguise: libraries, classes, file layout, cache or queue products. Name a
  technology only as a `CON-n` or a `DEC-n`.
- Padding: generic rows every system has, the catalog copied into the document, N/A sections.
- Invented numbers: a plausible p95 nobody confirmed. Ask, and keep `proposed` until confirmed.
- Lowering a requirement to match today's code; restating scenarios as technical rows.
- Screening only the obvious codes: security, privacy and recoverability are the usual omissions.
- Ticket keys, request sentences, names, task progress or N/A verdicts in the tech design files.

